  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-19-east-new-york/">← Back to District 19 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 19 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 19. Navigating the special education system can be challenging. This directory is designed to provide you with the necessary contact information and essential guidance for advocating for your child's needs. Understanding the roles and responsibilities of each contact is key to effective communication and timely resolution of any issues.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 5 (Serving District 19)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>1665 St. Marks Ave, Brooklyn, NY 11233</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-240-3557">718-240-3557</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777; margin-top: 10px;"><strong>Note:</strong>  Contacting the CSE via your NYC Schools Account (NYCSA) is the most reliable method to ensure your communications are officially documented. Ensure your NYCSA account is active and that you check it regularly for updates.</p>
                <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #777;">This CSE handles cases for students generally aged 5-21. For younger children, see CPSE information below (if applicable). Understand that specific staff assignments within the CSE can change; always confirm the case manager assigned to your child.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt (date, time, method of contact, person contacted) and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep a detailed log of all communications, as this will be crucial if you need to file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED).
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(iii)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.  This is critical for ensuring your child's rights are protected and that the district adheres to legally mandated timeframes.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary, reiterating the key points discussed and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use a clear and concise subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"Request for IEP Meeting - [Student Name]"</em>. Be specific about the purpose of your communication.</li>
                <li>Keep a detailed "Communication Log" of dates, times, methods of contact (phone, email, in-person), names of individuals contacted, and a brief summary of the conversation. This log is invaluable evidence if disputes arise.</li>
                <li>For important requests, consider sending a letter via certified mail with return receipt requested to ensure proof of delivery.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, or if you are dissatisfied with the response, follow this escalation ladder. Remember to document each step meticulously.  Lack of response can be a denial of Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request and the reason for your communication.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and any relevant related service providers).  Politely reiterate your request and mention that you are following up on your previous communication.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.  Attach copies of your previous emails and explain that you have not received a response despite multiple attempts. State that you are concerned about the lack of communication and its potential impact on your child's education.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or if the district fails to follow mandated procedures (8 NYCRR §200.5(l)).  A state complaint is a formal written complaint alleging a violation of a requirement of Part 200 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education or federal special education law.  Ensure your complaint is specific, detailed, and supported by documentation.  You can find the complaint form on the NYSED website. Consider consulting with a special education attorney before filing a state complaint.</li>
            </ol>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">Remember that NYSED has a specific timeframe for investigating and resolving state complaints. Familiarize yourself with these timelines to ensure your complaint is handled efficiently.</p>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 19
    </h2>
    
    <div class="advocacy-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Understanding Your Child's IEP</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The Individualized Education Program (IEP) is the cornerstone of your child's special education.  Ensure you thoroughly understand every aspect of the IEP, including goals, services, accommodations, and modifications.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Request a draft IEP prior to the IEP meeting to allow sufficient time for review (8 NYCRR §200.4(d)(2)(i)(a)).</li>
                <li>Come to the IEP meeting prepared with questions and concerns.</li>
                <li>Don't be afraid to disagree with the CSE if you believe the IEP does not adequately address your child's needs.</li>
                <li>Document any disagreements in writing and request that they be included in the IEP.</li>
                <li>Remember you have the right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)).</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain a comprehensive file of all documents related to your child's special education, including evaluations, IEPs, progress reports, correspondence, and meeting notes.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Organize your documents chronologically.</li>
                <li>Keep a log of all communications with school staff.</li>
                <li>Back up your files electronically.</li>
                <li>This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child's rights or file a complaint.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), New York State Part 200 Regulations, and relevant NYSED policies.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>IDEA guarantees your child the right to a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE) in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE).</li>
                <li>Part 200 Regulations outline the specific procedures and requirements for special education in New York State.</li>
                <li>NYSED provides various resources and guidance documents on its website.</li>
                <li>Consider attending workshops or training sessions on special education law and advocacy.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="advocacy-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Build Relationships</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                While it's essential to be assertive in advocating for your child's needs, building positive relationships with school staff can be beneficial.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Communicate respectfully and professionally.</li>
                <li>Express your appreciation for their efforts.</li>
                <li>Collaborate to find solutions that meet your child's needs.</li>
                <li>Remember that you and the school staff share a common goal: to help your child succeed.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) handles special education services for students aged 5-21. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) handles services for children aged 3-5. If your child is transitioning from preschool to kindergarten, their case will move from the CPSE to the CSE.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the CSE's recommendations?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">You have the right to disagree with the CSE's recommendations. Document your concerns in writing and request that they be included in the IEP. You also have the right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the district's evaluation (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)). You can also pursue mediation or file a complaint with NYSED.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">How often will my child's IEP be reviewed?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Your child's IEP must be reviewed at least annually (8 NYCRR §200.4(e)). However, you can request an IEP meeting at any time if you believe your child's needs are not being met.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #333;">What is FAPE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">FAPE stands for Free Appropriate Public Education. It is a right guaranteed to all children with disabilities under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). FAPE means that your child is entitled to special education and related services that are designed to meet their unique needs and prepare them for further education, employment, and independent living.</p>
    </div>
</section>


    </div>
</main>
